    m("lidocaine", "Lidocaine (Lignocaine)", "Lidocaine Hydrochloride 2%", "Lidocaine hydrochloride", "Lidocaine hydrochloride", "Antiarrhythmics",
      "The quintessential Class IB antiarrhythmic and amide local anesthetic, administered intravenously for acute ventricular arrhythmias during myocardial infarction or cardiac arrest, and topically for regional anesthesia.",
      ["2% IV vial (20 mg/mL)", "1% IV vial (10 mg/mL)", "2% topical gel", "5% transdermal patch", "10% aerosol spray"],
      ["Solution for Injection", "Topical Gel", "Transdermal Patch", "Aerosol Spray"], ["Intravenous", "Topical", "Infiltration / Nerve Block"],
      "Binds to the intracellular pore of voltage-gated fast sodium channels (Nav1.5) in cardiac tissue, preferentially binding to open and inactivated channels in depolarized, ischemic, or rapidly firing myocardium; shortens action potential duration and effective refractory period.",
      "Suppresses abnormal ventricular automaticity and re-entrant circuits in ischemic myocardium without depressing AV nodal conduction or cardiac contractility; topically blocks sodium channels in sensory nerve fibers, producing reversible local anesthesia.",
      "Negligible oral bioavailability (<3%) due to massive hepatic first-pass metabolism; administered parenterally or topically.",
      "Rapidly distributes into well-perfused organs (brain, heart, liver), followed by muscle and fat; volume of distribution approx. 1.5 L/kg; 60-80% bound to plasma alpha-1 acid glycoprotein.",
      "Extensively metabolized in the liver (>95%) by CYP3A4 and CYP1A2 to active de-ethylated metabolites (monoethylglycinexylidide - MEGX, and glycinexylidide - GX) which possess convulsant and antiarrhythmic properties.",
      "Excreted in urine (approx. 90% as metabolites; less than 5% as unchanged parent drug).",
      "<3% (oral); 100% (IV)", "1.5 to 2 hours (prolonged to 4-10 hours in heart failure, cardiogenic shock, or severe hepatic disease)", "60% to 80%", "Immediate (IV bolus: 45 to 90 seconds)", "10 to 20 minutes after single IV bolus; requires continuous infusion",
      ["Acute treatment of ventricular tachycardia (VT) and ventricular fibrillation (VF) during cardiac arrest (alternative to amiodarone)", "Ventricular arrhythmias complicating acute myocardial infarction or cardiac surgery", "Local and regional infiltration anesthesia, peripheral nerve blocks, and epidural anesthesia", "Post-herpetic neuralgia (5% lidocaine transdermal patch)", "Topical anesthesia of mucosal membranes"],
      ["Severe sinoatrial, atrioventricular, or intraventricular heart block without pacemaker", "Stokes-Adams syndrome or Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome", "Known hypersensitivity to amide-type local anesthetics"],
      ["LOCAL ANESTHETIC SYSTEMIC TOXICITY (LAST): Accidental intravenous injection during nerve block or excessive infusion causes CNS toxicity (circumoral numbness, tinnitus, metallic taste, seizures) followed by cardiovascular collapse; treat immediately with 20% INTRAVENOUS LIPID EMULSION", "Hepatic clearance is blood-flow dependent: drastically reduce maintenance infusion rate in heart failure, cardiogenic shock, or cirrhosis", "Narrow therapeutic window: therapeutic serum levels 1.5 to 5.0 mcg/mL (toxic > 5.0 mcg/mL)"],
      ["Drowsiness / sedation", "Dizziness", "Metallic taste", "Circumoral paresthesias (tingling around lips and tongue - warning sign of toxicity)", "Tinnitus (ringing in ears)"],
      ["Grand mal seizures / convulsions", "Respiratory depression / arrest", "Cardiovascular collapse / asystole", "Severe malignant hyperthermia (rare)"],
      ["Beta-blockers (propranolol/metoprolol decrease hepatic blood flow, increasing lidocaine levels and toxicity)", "Cimetidine (inhibits CYP3A4, increasing lidocaine levels)", "Amiodarone (additive myocardial depression and neurotoxicity)"],
      ["Continuous ECG telemetry and automated blood pressure monitoring during IV infusion", "Serum lidocaine levels if infusion exceeds 24 hours (therapeutic range: 1.5 to 5.0 mcg/mL)", "Monitor mental status for early neurotoxicity (tinnitus, slurred speech, confusion)"],
      "Category B; widely used for epidural and infiltration anesthesia in labor and delivery with excellent safety.",
      "Excreted in breast milk in low levels; compatible with breastfeeding.",
      "Approved in pediatric resuscitation (1 mg/kg IV/IO initial bolus, followed by 20-50 mcg/kg/min infusion).",
      "Lower volume of distribution and reduced hepatic clearance; decrease initial bolus and maintenance infusion by 50%.",
      "No dosage adjustment for initial bolus; metabolites accumulate in severe renal failure with prolonged infusion (>24h).",
      "Reduce maintenance infusion rate by 50% in severe hepatic disease or cardiogenic shock (decreased liver perfusion).",
      ["Rapid onset within 60 to 90 seconds for acute ventricular arrhythmias in the ICU or cardiac arrest", "Does not worsen AV nodal conduction or reduce systemic blood pressure compared to amiodarone", "Universal versatile local anesthetic available in gels, sprays, patches, and injectables"],
      ["Zero oral bioavailability (requires continuous IV infusion)", "High risk of central nervous system toxicity and seizures with rapid infusions or overdose"],
      ["First sign of lidocaine toxicity is numbness around the lips, metallic taste, and ringing in the ears", "Rescue antidote for accidental overdose/cardiac arrest is 20% Intravenous Lipid Emulsion"],
      "LIDO-caine: Local & IV antiarrhythmic; Ischemic tissue preference; Danger: Overdose causes Seizures (treat with Lipids).", "-caine", "LYE-doe-kane", "लिडो-केन"),

