    m("rivaroxaban", "Rivaroxaban", "Rivaroxaban", "Rivaroxaban", "Rivaroxaban", "Anticoagulants",
      "An oral direct factor Xa inhibitor (DOAC) taken once daily with the evening meal for stroke prevention in non-valvular atrial fibrillation and treatment/prevention of deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism.",
      ["2.5 mg", "10 mg", "15 mg", "20 mg"], ["Film-coated Tablet"], ["Oral"],
      "Selectively, reversibly, and competitively inhibits free and clot-bound factor Xa and prothrombinase activity; blocks the intrinsic and extrinsic coagulation pathways, preventing thrombin generation and fibrin clot propagation.",
      "Produces predictable anticoagulation without requiring routine coagulation monitoring (INR); prolonged anti-Xa effect provides reliable 24-hour thromboprophylaxis.",
      "Rapidly absorbed; peak plasma concentration in 2 to 4 hours; bioavailability for 10 mg is 80-100% (unaffected by food); bioavailability for 15 mg and 20 mg is approx. 66% fasting, but increases to near 100% WHEN TAKEN WITH FOOD (mandatory to take 15 mg and 20 mg with a meal).",
      "Apparent volume of distribution approx. 50 L; high plasma protein binding (92-95%, predominantly albumin).",
      "Metabolized in the liver (approx. 66%) via CYP3A4, CYP2J2, and CYP-independent hydrolysis.",
      "Eliminated by dual pathways: approx. 36% excreted unchanged in urine; remainder excreted as inactive metabolites in urine (approx. 30%) and faeces (approx. 30%).",
      "80% to 100% (10 mg); 15 mg and 20 mg MUST BE TAKEN WITH FOOD to achieve 100% bioavailability", "5 to 9 hours in young adults; 11 to 13 hours in elderly patients", "92% to 95%", "2 to 4 hours", "24 hours",
      ["Stroke and systemic embolism prevention in non-valvular atrial fibrillation (20 mg once daily with the evening meal)", "Treatment of deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary embolism (PE) (15 mg BID with food for 21 days, then 20 mg once daily with food)", "Secondary prevention of recurrent DVT and PE (10 mg or 20 mg once daily)", "Prophylaxis of DVT/PE following elective hip or knee replacement surgery (10 mg once daily)", "Reduction of major cardiovascular events in stable CAD or PAD (2.5 mg twice daily combined with aspirin - COMPASS trial)"],
      ["Active clinically significant pathological bleeding (intracranial hemorrhage, active GI bleed)", "Lesion or condition carrying significant risk of major bleeding (recent gastrointestinal ulceration, brain neoplasm)", "Concomitant treatment with other anticoagulants", "Hepatic disease associated with coagulopathy and clinically relevant bleeding risk (Child-Pugh B and C)", "Pregnancy and breastfeeding", "Prosthetic mechanical heart valves or moderate-to-severe mitral stenosis"],
      ["Black box warning: Premature discontinuation of rivaroxaban in atrial fibrillation increases the risk of thrombotic stroke (always provide bridge coverage if discontinued)", "Black box warning: Epidural or spinal hematomas can occur in patients receiving neuraxial anesthesia or spinal puncture, leading to long-term or permanent paralysis (delay catheter removal based on drug half-life)", "FOOD REQUIREMENT: The 15 mg and 20 mg tablets MUST be taken with food; fasting drops bioavailability by 35%, leading to under-anticoagulation and stroke"],
      ["Bleeding (epistaxis, gingival bleeding, bruising, hematuria, gastrointestinal bleeding)", "Nausea", "Dyspepsia", "Headache", "Elevated transaminases"],
      ["Fatal or major intracranial hemorrhage", "Retroperitoneal or gastrointestinal bleeding", "Spinal / epidural hematoma with paralysis", "Severe thrombocytopenia (rare)"],
      ["Combined P-gp and strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, itraconazole, ritonavir - avoid combination due to high bleeding risk)", "Combined P-gp and strong CYP3A4 inducers (rifampin, carbamazepine, phenytoin - reduce rivaroxaban levels by 50%; avoid)", "Antiplatelets, NSAIDs, SSRIs (significantly increase gastrointestinal bleeding risk)"],
      ["Renal function (creatinine clearance via Cockcroft-Gault equation) at baseline and at least annually", "Complete blood count (hemoglobin and hematocrit) to detect occult bleeding", "Specific reversal agent: Andexanet alfa (approved for life-threatening Factor Xa bleeding)"],
      "Contraindicated in pregnancy; crosses placenta; causes maternal bleeding and fetal harm.",
      "Contraindicated during breastfeeding; excreted in human breast milk.",
      "Approved for treatment of venous thromboembolism in pediatric patients (weight-adjusted oral suspension).",
      "Higher plasma levels due to age-related decline in renal function; evaluate bleeding risk.",
      "Dose adjustment based on CrCl: CrCl 15-50 mL/min: reduce dose to 15 mg once daily with the evening meal; CrCl < 15 mL/min: avoid use.",
      "Contraindicated in Child-Pugh B and C hepatic impairment or any liver disease with preexisting coagulopathy.",
      ["Convenient once-daily dosing with the evening meal for atrial fibrillation", "Does not require routine INR blood monitoring or dietary vitamin K restrictions (unlike warfarin)", "Lower risk of fatal intracranial hemorrhage compared to warfarin in clinical trials", "Specific reversal agent (Andexanet alfa) available for emergencies"],
      ["The 15 mg and 20 mg doses MUST be taken with food or absorption drops drastically", "Slightly higher rate of gastrointestinal bleeding than dabigatran or apixaban"],
      ["CRITICAL RULE: The 15 mg and 20 mg tablets MUST be taken with dinner (food is required for absorption)", "Never stop this medication without consulting your doctor (sudden stopping causes strokes)"],
      "RIVAROXA-ban: Reversible Inactivator of Xa; Administer with evening Meal; Beware stopping suddenly.", "-xaban", "ri-va-ROX-a-ban", "रिवारोक्सा-बैन"),

